          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "When someone says they have heart failure, does that mean the heart has stopped working?",
          "answer": "No, not in the usual sense. The name \"heart failure\" is misleading. It does not mean the heart has stopped or is about to stop. The condition is a long-term one, often managed for many years. It means the heart cannot fill with blood or pump it out well enough to meet the body's needs. (Source: \"Heart failure,\" general clinical reference.)\n\nThe main way doctors describe it is by ejection fraction. This is the share of blood in the heart's main pumping chamber that is pushed out with each beat. The groups are:\n\n- **Reduced**: 40 percent or below. The heart muscle contracts weakly.\n- **Mildly reduced**: 41 to 49 percent.\n- **Preserved**: 50 percent or above. Here the heart muscle squeezes normally, but the chamber stiffens and does not fill well. Preserved ejection fraction is not a milder form. It is a different problem, and it makes up about half of cases. (Source: \"Heart failure,\" general clinical reference; \"Heart failure with reduced ejection fraction,\" cardiology guidance.)\n\nCoding systems also sort heart failure by mechanism (systolic or diastolic) and by how sudden it is (acute, chronic, or both). The sources I found do not give a severity scale such as mild, moderate or severe, so I cannot describe one from them.\n\nTreatment differs between these groups, so the exact type matters. A clinician who has examined the person and reviewed their test results is the right person to say which type applies. If someone has sudden severe breathlessness, chest pain, or fainting, they should seek emergency care straight away.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
